    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <exercises>
       
    <example>
        <title>Calculating the Height of a Building</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                A surveyor stands <m>50\text{ m}</m> away from the base of a building. The angle of elevation to the top of the building is <m>35°</m>. Find the height of the building.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We have a right-angled triangle where:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>Adjacent side (horizontal distance) = <m>50\text{ m}</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Angle of elevation = <m>35°</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Opposite side (height) = <m>h</m> (unknown)</p></li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Using the tangent ratio:
            </p>
            <md>
                <mrow>\tan(35°) = \frac{h}{50}</mrow>
                <mrow>h = 50 \times \tan(35°)</mrow>
                <mrow>h = 50 \times 0.7002</mrow>
                <mrow>h \approx 35.01\text{ m}</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>
                Therefore, the height of the building is approximately <m>35\text{ m}</m>.
            </p>
        </solution> 
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Finding the Distance Across a River</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                An engineer needs to find the width of a river. She stands at point <m>A</m> on one bank and spots a tree at point <m>B</m> on the opposite bank. She then walks <m>100\text{ m}</m> along the riverbank to point <m>C</m>. From <m>C</m>, the angle to the tree is <m>40°</m>. The angle at <m>A</m> between the riverbank and the line to the tree is <m>90°</m>. Find the width of the river.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We have a right-angled triangle where:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>Point <m>A</m> is on the riverbank</p></li>
                <li><p>Point <m>C</m> is <m>100\text{ m}</m> along the bank from <m>A</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Point <m>B</m> is the tree across the river</p></li>
                <li><p>The angle at <m>A</m> is <m>90°</m></p></li>
                <li><p>The angle at <m>C</m> is <m>40°</m></p></li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The width of the river is the perpendicular distance from <m>B</m> to the line <m>AC</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                In the right triangle, using the tangent ratio from point <m>C</m>:
            </p>
            <md>
                <mrow>\tan(40°) = \frac{AB}{AC}</mrow>
                <mrow>AB = AC \times \tan(40°)</mrow>
                <mrow>AB = 100 \times 0.8391</mrow>
                <mrow>AB \approx 83.91\text{ m}</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>
                Therefore, the width of the river is approximately <m>84\text{ m}</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Navigation: Finding Distance Between Two Ships</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                Two ships leave a port at the same time. Ship <m>A</m> travels at a bearing of <m>30°</m> for <m>50\text{ km}</m>. Ship <m>B</m> travels at a bearing of <m>120°</m> for <m>70\text{ km}</m>. Find the distance between the two ships.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The angle between the two bearings is:
            </p>
            <md>
                <mrow>120° - 30° = 90°</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>
                We have a triangle where:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>Side <m>a</m> (Ship A's distance) = <m>50\text{ km}</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Side <m>b</m> (Ship B's distance) = <m>70\text{ km}</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Included angle <m>C</m> = <m>90°</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Side <m>c</m> (distance between ships) = unknown</p></li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Using the cosine rule:
            </p>
            <md>
                <mrow>c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2ab\cos C</mrow>
                <mrow>c^2 = 50^2 + 70^2 - 2(50)(70)\cos(90°)</mrow>
                <mrow>c^2 = 2500 + 4900 - 7000(0)</mrow>
                <mrow>c^2 = 7400</mrow>
                <mrow>c = \sqrt{7400}</mrow>
                <mrow>c \approx 86.02\text{ km}</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>
                Therefore, the distance between the two ships is approximately <m>86\text{ km}</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Roofing: Finding the Angle of a Roof Section</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                A roof has two support beams that meet at a point. The first beam is <m>8\text{ m}</m> long, the second beam is <m>6\text{ m}</m> long, and the distance between their endpoints is <m>10\text{ m}</m>. Find the angle between the two beams where they meet.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We have a triangle where:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>Side <m>a</m> (first beam) = <m>8\text{ m}</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Side <m>b</m> (second beam) = <m>6\text{ m}</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Side <m>c</m> (distance between endpoints) = <m>10\text{ m}</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Angle <m>C</m> (angle between beams) = unknown</p></li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Using the rearranged cosine rule:
            </p>
            <md>
                <mrow>\cos C = \frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}</mrow>
                <mrow>\cos C = \frac{8^2 + 6^2 - 10^2}{2(8)(6)}</mrow>
                <mrow>\cos C = \frac{64 + 36 - 100}{96}</mrow>
                <mrow>\cos C = \frac{0}{96} = 0</mrow>
                <mrow>C = \cos^{-1}(0) = 90°</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>
                Therefore, the angle between the two beams is <m>90°</m> (a right angle). This is a right-angled triangle, which follows the Pythagorean theorem: <m>8^2 + 6^2 = 64 + 36 = 100 = 10^2</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Land Surveying: Finding the Area of a Triangular Plot</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                A surveyor measures a triangular plot of land. The three sides are <m>120\text{ m}</m>, <m>150\text{ m}</m>, and <m>180\text{ m}</m>. First, find the largest angle, then calculate the area of the plot.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <strong>Step 1: Find the largest angle.</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                The largest angle is opposite the longest side. So we find the angle opposite the <m>180\text{ m}</m> side.
            </p>
            <p>
                Let <m>a = 120\text{ m}</m>, <m>b = 150\text{ m}</m>, <m>c = 180\text{ m}</m>, and angle <m>C</m> is opposite side <m>c</m>.
            </p>
            <md>
                <mrow>\cos C = \frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}</mrow>
                <mrow>\cos C = \frac{120^2 + 150^2 - 180^2}{2(120)(150)}</mrow>
                <mrow>\cos C = \frac{14400 + 22500 - 32400}{36000}</mrow>
                <mrow>\cos C = \frac{4500}{36000} = 0.125</mrow>
                <mrow>C = \cos^{-1}(0.125) \approx 82.8°</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>
                <strong>Step 2: Calculate the area.</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                Using the formula: <m>\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2}ab\sin C</m>
            </p>
            <md>
                <mrow>\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2}(120)(150)\sin(82.8°)</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2}(120)(150)(0.9903)</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{Area} = 9000 \times 0.9903</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{Area} \approx 8912.7\text{ m}^2</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>
                Therefore, the largest angle is approximately <m>82.8°</m>, and the area of the triangular plot is approximately <m>8913\text{ m}^2</m> (or <m>0.89\text{ hectares}</m>).
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
  
    </exercises>

